nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Further, I particularly dislike balanced hands with 11 HCP. They look better than they usually turn out to be.|
PG| ***** 19 ***** |


mb|1C|mb|P|mb|1D|mb|P|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|mb|1H|mb|P|mb|1N|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Good bidding by North. Technically North does have 10 Bergen Points - 9 HCP, 1 length point, 1 quality suit point, -1 for Jx of clubs. |
PG| ***** 20 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|And, since South opened 1@C, you could decide not to deduct 1 for Jx of clubs, which would give North 11 points, enough to bid 2N. But I think 1N is the better choice since it is unlikely partner has enough for their side to be in game. |
PG| ***** 21 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Additionally, their side is not likely to have a good fit since just about all of North’s honors are outside South’s suits, And there is even some chance that 2N might go down.|
PG| ***** 22 ***** |


mb|P|mb|P|mb|P|
pg| ***** 23 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|
pc|H6|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|It is unclear what East should lead. The problem with a heart lead is you generally do not want to lead suits that the opponents have bid especially when the suit is behind you and as you can see, it will establish a 2nd heart trick.|
PG| ***** 24 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Personally, I would have led the S9 which turns out to be a neutral lead, though, as I mentioned it is very unclear what to lead.|
